{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me start by reading the SPEC.md file and the plan-template skill file to understand what's needed.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Let me start by reading the SPEC.md file and the plan-template skill file to understand what's needed.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_91c78c49aace48c4add815ca","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_178c6649194c42758ecc565f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/opt/watts/kit/skills/plan-template/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":2455,"output":115,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":21,"totalTokens":2570,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791815472143,"responseId":"gen-1791815472-bh0db50GItLbJGKLjZWQ","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_91c78c49aace48c4add815ca","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Tic-tac-toe — minimal terminal game for two human players\n\n## Status\nDraft\nVersion: v1\nOwner: <to be assigned>\nLast updated: <date>\n\n## Intent\nTwo local human players need a terminal-based tic-tac-toe game they can run without installing anything beyond Go. The game prints the board each turn, accepts moves by cell number, enforces the rules, and announces the outcome — a win, a draw, or an abandoned session (EOF) — then stops. No AI, web server, persistence, or configuration framework is needed.\n\n## Background and context\nThe workspace is empty; no Go project exists yet. This is a greenfield implementation of one of the simplest possible interactive terminal games. The human request supplies all the rules, constraints, and scope boundaries needed. The implementation is expected to be a single `main.go` plus focused test files, with a `go.mod` at the project root. A lint gate (configured elsewhere) will run alongside `go test ./...`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- AC-1: When the program starts, the system shall print an empty 3×3 board labelled with cell numbers 1–9 and prompt Player X for a move.\n- AC-2: When a player enters a valid unoccupied cell number (1–9), the system shall place that player's mark, update the board display, and switch to the other player's turn.\n- AC-3: If a player enters a cell number that is already occupied, then the system shall print an error message, display the current board again, and keep the same player's turn without changing the board state.\n- AC-4: If a player enters a number outside the range 1–9, then the system shall print an error message and prompt the same player again without changing the board state.\n- AC-5: If a player enters nonnumeric input (text, special characters, etc.), then the system shall print an error message and prompt the same player again without changing the board state.\n- AC-6: After any move, if the board contains three matching marks in any row, column, or diagonal, the system shall announce the winning player and exit.\n- AC-7: After any move, if the board is full with no win, the system shall announce a draw and exit.\n- AC-8: When the program reads EOF (e.g., Ctrl+D / Ctrl+Z) at any input prompt, the system shall print an exit message and terminate cleanly with a zero exit code.\n- AC-9: When the program runs `go test ./...`, all tests shall pass.\n- AC-10: When the configured lint gate runs against the project, it shall report zero errors.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\n- No AI opponent or computer player.\n- No network, web server, or API.\n- No persistence (file storage, database, leaderboard, save/load).\n- No configuration files, environment variables, or CLI flags beyond what Go's standard library supplies.\n- No external application dependencies beyond the Go standard library.\n- No concurrency, goroutines, or channels.\n- No GUI, ncurses, or interactive TUI framework — terminal line I/O only.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The players are co-located at the same terminal and take turns at the same keyboard.\n- The `go.mod` file will declare the module path `tic-tac-toe` (or a name specified by the developer).\n- A configured linter (e.g., `golangci-lint`) is expected to pass; no specific linter config is supplied in this spec.\n- Go is already installed in the build environment.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Use only the Go standard library; no third-party packages.\n- The implementation must be readable and small — ideally one `main.go` plus separate test files.\n- Application files (`go.mod`, `*.go`) must be created in the project root (`/workspace`), not in the task directory (`/runtime/task`).\n- Zero lint errors per the project's configured lint gate.\n\n## Open questions\n\nNone. The human request, the acceptance criteria above, and the explicit non-goals cover all known decisions. Cell display format (e.g., ASCII grid with `|` and `-`) and exact error-message wording are left to the implementation, as they do not affect correctness.\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n\n- Touches: A new, standalone Go terminal application with no network access, no file I/O beyond terminal stdin/stdout, and no external dependencies.\n- Does not touch: Any existing production code, credentials, entitlements, identity, network services, persistent storage, or configuration infrastructure.\n\n## Risk tier\n0\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The human approves or rejects it at the spec-review workflow gate with `wctl task decide <task> spec-review`, optionally using `--reject --feedback 'changes needed'`."}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815475755},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_178c6649194c42758ecc565f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: plan-template\ndescription: Produces PLAN.md from the bundled template once the spec-review workflow gate is approved. Use during Design and Architecture to define a technical approach, data model, risk tier recheck, quality gates, and the user stories the build loop will work through.\n---\n\n# Plan Template\n\nCopy `plan-template.md`, bundled in this skill's folder, into the task directory as `PLAN.md`, then fill it in against SPEC.md approved by the preceding workflow gate. `wctl task new <slug>` puts a copy in place. The spec-review workflow gate must approve the specification before planning starts; approval lives in workflow history, not in an agent-written signature.\n\nThe project root is the current working directory containing watts.yaml. Implement application source, tests, modules, and other project files in the project root and its normal source directories. The task directory holds workflow documents, story logs, review artifacts, and execution evidence only; do not create application code or a separate application module inside it. Quality gates run from the project root, not with a task-directory working directory.\n\nRules worth repeating here rather than assuming they're obvious:\n\n- Every section needs an actual answer, not a placeholder. \"None\" is a valid answer for Data Model and Interface Changes, a blank field is not, a blank field is indistinguishable from forgetting to check.\n- Technical Decisions and Alternatives Considered is not allowed to collapse silently for anything above Tier 0. If there's nothing in it, that's worth noticing, not skipping past.\n- Risk Tier Recheck is mandatory, not a formality. Run the risk-tiering skill again against what this plan actually describes, not what SPEC.md assumed before the technical approach was known. A tier can move up here. It should never move down without saying why.\n- Open Questions for the author agent exists so ambiguity gets resolved here, in writing, rather than surfacing for the first time as an admission-rubric rejection.\n- The planning agent drafts this file. The default SDLC requires a human plan-review gate before implementation at every tier.\n\n## The two sections the build loop runs on\n\nThere is no separate task list. The build loop (pi-ralph-loop) reads this file every iteration, and scripts parse two sections mechanically, so their format is fixed.\n\nQuality gates:\n\n- One command per bullet, written as `- \\`command\\``, for example `- \\`go test ./...\\``. These are real commands for this repository, run from the repository root.\n- The builder runs them before finishing a story, and the reviewer and the checks run them again independently. Avoid network access, `sudo`, `curl`, `ssh` and Git commands in a gate. `gates.sh` refuses commands that look dangerous.\n\nUser stories:\n\n- Each story is a heading `### US-001: Short title`, numbered in the order the builder should take them.\n- Each story has a `Covers: AC-1, AC-2` line naming the SPEC criteria it satisfies, and a `Depends on:` line (`none` or story ids).\n- Every acceptance criterion in SPEC.md must be covered by at least one story, or `check-plan.sh` fails.\n- Each story must be small enough for one agent iteration and have checkable acceptance criteria. Nothing downstream splits a story that is too big, so split it here. A story that cannot finish in one iteration will fail repeatedly.\n- Put the riskiest or approach-proving story first, and never make a story depend on a later one.\n\n## Fill-in rules the Watts checks read\n\n- `Risk tier after planning: N` in the Risk Tier Recheck section, where N is `0`, `1`, or `2`. The higher of this and SPEC.md's tier governs.\n- `Spec ref: SPEC.md vN` near the top must match SPEC.md's `Version:`, or the approval is treated as stale.\n- The plan-review human workflow gate records approval of the specification and plan hashes. An agent never fills in a human signature or approves itself.\n\nAfter writing PLAN.md, the agent returns. The workflow pauses at plan-review; approval continues to build and rejection returns to planning with feedback.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815475757}]}
